</div></div></div><div><div data-block-type="2" id="block-yui_3_17_2_1_1688473488503_16044"><div>

<div>
  <p>The Polish newspaper Nasz Dziennik <a href="https://www.radiomaryja.pl/bez-kategorii/widzielismy-serce-w-hostii-z-sokolki/" target="_blank">interviewed Sobaniec-Łotowska and Sulkowski</a> in December 2009:</p><blockquote><p><em>Sulkowski: If we put the Communion wafer in the water, in the normal course of events it should dissolve in a short time. In this case, however, part of the Communion, for some incomprehensible reason, did not dissolve. Moreover, what is even more incomprehensible—the tissue that appeared on the Communion was tightly connected to it—infiltrating the substrate on which it was formed. Take my word for it that even if someone had intended to manipulate it, he would not have been able to connect the two structures so inseparably.</em></p></blockquote><p>Sulkowski found two things astounding about this sample. First, the Communion wafer, which contains only wheat flour and water, did not decompose, as any unleavened bread soaked in water would do after 18 days. Second, the bread and cardiac muscle tissues were intricately interwoven in a way that would be impossible to accomplish through human manipulation.</p><blockquote><p><em>Sobaniec-Łotowska: This remarkable phenomenon of the intermingling of the Communion and the fibers of the heart muscle observed in both light microscopes and transmission electron microscopy also demonstrates to me that there could be no human interference here. In addition, please note another unusual phenomenon. The Communion stayed in the water for a long time, and then even longer on the corporal. Thus, the tissue that appeared in the Communion should have undergone a process of autolysis [Editor’s note: a type of necrosis]. Examining the collected material, we found no such changes. I think that at the current stage of development of knowledge, we are not able to explain the studied phenomenon solely based on natural science.</em></p></blockquote><p>Transmission electron microscopy, a tool far more powerful than light microscopy, is used to visualize incredibly small details, including viral particles and atoms. After using this sensitive tool, Sobaniec-Łotowska agreed with Sulkowski’s assessment of the interwoven fibers. This integration could not have been achieved by any human craft. She also affirmed that the cardiac tissue should’ve decomposed as any fleshy tissue would when separated from a living body. Instead, the cardiac tissue remained intact without any signs of degradation.</p><blockquote><p><em>Sobaniec-Łotowska: Together, we would like to highlight the fact that in the case of the Communion fragment we studied, it was possible to observe so many morphologically characteristic markers indicative of myocardial tissue in such a small amount of collected material. One such marker is the phenomenon of segmentation, i.e., damage to the myocardial fibers at the site of the insertions [Editor’s note: structures characteristic of the heart muscle] and the phenomenon of fragmentation. This damage is visible as small cracks. Such changes occur only in non-necrotic fibers and reflect the rapid contraction of the heart muscle in the premortem agonal period.</em></p></blockquote><p>The two professors noted fragmentation in the heart tissue. Fragmentation indicates that the heart is undergoing extreme stress while the heart cells are still alive. This finding would not occur after the tissue had already died, suggesting that the body the tissue came from had been suffering while still alive.</p><blockquote><p><em>Sobaniec-Łotowska: Another important piece of evidence that the examined material could be human heart muscle was the mainly central arrangement of cell nuclei in the observed fibers, which is a characteristic phenomenon for this muscle. On the course of some fibers, we also found images that could correspond to nodes of contraction. On the other hand, electron microscope examination showed outlines of insertions and bundles of delicate myofibrils. In the summary of the expertise carried out, in the protocol submitted to the archdiocesan curia, we stated: “The material sent for evaluation [...] in the opinion of two independent pathomorphologists is satisfactory; it indicates myocardial tissue, or at least of all the living tissues of the body, that most closely resembles it.” And what we think is important: the material we analyzed is this tissue in its entirety.</em></p></blockquote><p>After independently examining this tissue with light and electron microscopes, both professors concluded that the sampled tissue was heart muscle.</p><p>Because of these astonishing findings, Sobaniec-Łotowska and Sulkowski were formally reprimanded by their university and accused of carrying out “illegal” and “disloyal” investigations that incorporated the “emotional” aspect of their Catholic faith. A<a href="https://www.se.pl/wiadomosci/polska/cud-w-sokoce-to-oszustwo-aa-G2o6-EMv7-X3ds.html" target="_blank"><span> tabloid magazine article</span></a> speculated that the red substance might have been bacterial contamination with Serratia marcescens, even though these rod-shaped bacteria do not resemble heart muscle tissue under the microscope. The president of the Polish Rationalist Association even initiated a public lawsuit calling for the district attorney of Sokolka to launch a criminal investigation for murder since the heart tissue must have come from someone who was no longer alive.</p><p>Sulkowski defended what he did:</p><blockquote><p><em>We have the duty to investigate every scientific problem… Just as a doctor cannot refuse to care for a patient, likewise, we have the duty to research every scientific problem, according to the guidelines of the Polish Academy of Sciences.</em></p></blockquote><p>The problem with their report was that, as Sobaniec-Łotowska stated in her interview, “we are not able to explain the studied phenomenon solely based on natural science.” Why did the host not dissolve after 18 days in water? Why did the heart muscle not show any autolytic changes or other signs of decomposition after being immersed as well? How did the muscle and host become intertwined in such a fashion that two experts in the field independently concluded that a human could not have fabricated it? Science cannot currently offer satisfactory answers to these questions.</p><p>It is natural then to consider the possibility of fraud. In Sokolka, only two people had keys to access the container that held the transformed host. But let’s imagine that someone got access and wished to publicize a miracle to garner attention for themselves or the Church. It’s difficult to envision such a person going to the trouble—even if they had the ability—to fabricate a piece of heart tissue elaborately interwoven with bread in the anticipation that it would later be examined under an electron microscope.&nbsp;</p><p>Reporting these scientifically inexplicable findings only harmed Sobaniec-Łotowska and Sulkowski’s professional reputations at their university, so they lack any obvious motive for colluding or falsifying these strange results when both professors were already well-known and respected for publishing traditional scientific journal articles. On the contrary, it appears that their rigorous&nbsp; scientific approach convinced them to stand by their objective findings despite the surrounding controversy. Their results highlight both the usefulness of science in microscopically confirming a tissue’s identity and the limits of current science to explain everything. If this event was a Eucharistic miracle, these mystifying findings are part of the miracle.&nbsp;</p>
</div>



</div></div></div><div><div data-block-type="5" id="block-579caf048c70b2ddfc5b"><div>













































